TI  - Systematic review of character development in low- and middle-income countries
AU  - David Jeffery
AU  - Junying Li
AU  - Phalasha Nagpal
AU  - Tim Lomas
JO  - The Journal of Positive Psychology
PY  - 2024
VL  - 20
IS  - 1
SP  - 169
EP  - 191
DO  - 10.1080/17439760.2024.2322464
AB  - ABSTRACT Character strengths and development is one of the most important and influential areas of positive psychology. Yet most of this research involves – as per the field generally – research in high income countries, with relatively little awareness of its dynamics in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs). However, there is a burgeoning research base in such countries, but this has hitherto been neither consolidated nor widely disseminated. To remedy this lacuna, this paper reports on a systematic review of the peer-reviewed journal articles on character development in LMICs, analysing 164 articles selected from 1,507 initial abstracts. Our analysis shows that research into character in LMICs is developing rapidly, noting various significant trends with respect to countries, populations, outcomes, and interventions. We also point where further efforts are needed, showing how research on this important topic can continue to prosper in regions that have been relatively neglected in the literature.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Well-being, academic success, and the role of relationships
AU  - Lisette Wijnia
JO  - Educational Psychology
PY  - 2021
VL  - 41
IS  - 8
SP  - 949
EP  - 951
DO  - 10.1080/01443410.2021.1994305
AB  - Educational Psychology focuses on research on psychological aspects of education for students at different educational levels, with or without specific learning needs. The articles of the current issue all deal with student well-being, academic success, or the role of the relationships with significant others (e.g. peers, parents, teachers) on these outcomes. Student well-being receives much attention nowadays (e.g. Govorova et al., 2020; Pascoe et al., 2020). Promoting children and young people’s well-being is an important sustainable development goal (United Nations Educational & Scientific & Cultural Organization, 2016). Although definitions of student well-being differ, well-being is often operationalised as feelings of satisfaction, happiness, and positive affect (e.g. Headey et al., 1984; Parviainen et al., 2020; Ryff et al., 2006). In contrast, ill-being can be characterised by negative affect and psychological distress, such as school burnout. Several articles in this issue focus on explaining students’ wellor ill-being (Teuber et al., 2021; Vinter et al., 2021; Yotyodying et al., 2021). Yotyodying et al. (2021) conducted a longitudinal study into the detrimental role of punitive parenting (e.g. verbal aggression) on the psychological well-being of children with learning disabilities. Children’s perception of punitive parenting was associated with lower self-esteem, positive affect, and life satisfaction, as well as more negative affect when relationships were investigated crosssectionally. Furthermore, children’s perceptions of punitive parenting in the third grade were associated with greater negative affect in the fourth grade. This study’s results indicate that parents play a critical role in promoting students’ well-being. Two articles in this issue examined burnout among middle or high school students (Teuber et al., 2021; Vinter et al., 2021). School burnout consists of symptoms such as feelings of strain and chronic fatigue concerning school demands (i.e. exhaustion), an indifferent or distal attitude towards school (i.e. cynicism), and feelings of inadequacy at school (Salmela-Aro et al., 2009). Teuber et al. (2021) focussed on school emotional exhaustion and cynicism among Chinese high school students. Higher workload (i.e. self-reported time investment) and higher perceived academic demands were associated with more burnout symptoms. Results further showed that personal resources, such as self-efficacy and optimism, were associated with fewer burnout symptoms and may have a protective effect. The quality of teacher-student relationships and burnout symptoms did not significantly affect burnout symptoms in the overall model. Vinter et al. (2021) examined the association of cognitive emotion regulation strategies with academic burnout in Estonian middle school students. Their results showed that especially rumination, which concerns focussing on thoughts and feelings connected to unpleasant events, was associated with burnout. Together these studies suggest that to tackle burnout, maladaptive emotional regulation strategies such as rumination need to be addressed (Vinter et al., 2021) and student workload should be adequate (Teuber et al., 2021).
ER  - 

